      "lineAnnotations": {
        "0": {"synopsis":"The extension's main entry, $3000 (SYSTEM, then /12288): reserves its workspace\n(EXT_RESERVE_WS) and goes on at EXT_KEYB_SETUP with the cursor's blink on. The first of\nthree 3-byte entries at EXT_ON, EXT_ON_AGAIN and EXT_ON_DISPLAY; a fourth is at\nEXT_ON_NO_BLINK.\nObserved: the screen is cleared and shows BASIC ROM EXTENSION  2.51, then READY.","comment":"Reserve the 6-byte workspace"},
        "3": {"synopsis":"Entry $3003 (/12291): EXT_KEYB_SETUP without reserving a workspace, so it uses IY as it\nstands. It expects IY to point at a workspace already: EXT_RESERVE_WS leaves it there and\nBASIC never uses IY (no IY instruction in its decoded code).","comment":"Keyboard, display and RST $10 hooks"},
        "6": {"synopsis":"Entry $3006 (/12294), also the end of EXT_KEYB_SETUP: hooks the display driver\n(EXT_DISP_DRIVER) and RST $10 (EXT_NEXT_CHAR), then EXT_HOOK_VECTORS. Taken on its own it\nleaves BASIC's keyboard driver in place and reserves no workspace.","comment":"HL = the extension's display driver"},
        "9": {"comment":"Hook it and RST $10"},
        "11": {"synopsis":"Entry $300B (/12299): as EXT_ON, but with the workspace's blink flag (IY+6) at $10, so the\ncursor does not blink until SHIFT+BREAK turns blinking on.","comment":"Reserve the workspace"},
        "14": {"comment":"Blink flag $10: no blinking"},
        "18": {"synopsis":"Makes HL the display's driver (the display block's driver address at $401E), points RST\n$10's RAM vector ($4004) at EXT_NEXT_CHAR, clears the variables (CLEAR_VARS) and goes to\nBASIC_READY. Does not return.\nEntry: HL = the display driver.\nObserved: after /12288, $401E = $3147 (was DISPLAY_DRIVER) and $4004 = $33D4 (was\nNEXT_CHAR).","comment":"The display block's driver"},
        "21": {"comment":"RST $10's handler: EXT_NEXT_CHAR"},
        "24": {"comment":"RST $10's RAM vector"},
        "27": {"comment":"Clear the variables"},
        "30": {"comment":"To BASIC's prompt"},
        "33": {"synopsis":"Switches the keyboard driver over, with the cursor's blink on (A = 0):\n1. IY+6 = A (the blink flag), IY+1 to IY+5 = 0 (the key state; EXT_KEY_SCAN's first pass\n   without a key sets them to $FF, -, $FF, 3, 1).\n2. Clears the screen (CLS_CMD) and prints ENABLE_TEXT (PRINT_TEXT).\n3. The keyboard block's driver ($4016) = EXT_KEY_DRIVER.\n4. On at EXT_ON_DISPLAY for the display driver and the RST $10 hook.\nEntry: IY = the workspace.\nObserved: $4016 = EXT_KEY_DRIVER afterwards (was KEY_SCAN).","comment":"A = 0: the cursor blinks"},
        "34": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEYB_SETUP with the blink flag in A ($10 from EXT_ON_NO_BLINK: no blinking).","comment":"IY+6 = the blink flag"},
        "37": {"comment":"HL = the workspace"},
        "40": {"comment":"IY+1 to IY+5"},
        "43": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEYB_SETUP: zeroes IY+1 to IY+5, one per pass.","comment":"The next byte"},
        "44": {"comment":"Zero it"},
        "47": {"comment":"The ENABLE_TEXT message"},
        "50": {"comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "56": {"comment":"HL = EXT_KEY_DRIVER"},
        "59": {"comment":"The keyboard block's driver"},
        "62": {"comment":"On to the display driver"},
        "64": {"synopsis":"\"BASIC ROM EXTENSION  2.51  \" and the zero that ends it, printed by EXT_KEYB_SETUP through\nPRINT_TEXT after the screen is cleared. (Series I's extension prints NEW KEYBOARD ROUTINE\nENABLE here.)\nObserved: the line shows on the screen after SYSTEM /12288."},
        "92": {"synopsis":"Takes 6 bytes off the top of memory for the extension's workspace:\n1. The top of memory ($40B1) goes down by 6; the new top is also stored at $4049 and at\n   $401B, the keyboard block's last two bytes (\"KI\" at power-on). Nothing in BASIC reads\n   $4049 or $401B (no reference in its code); on the /64 the extension does not read them\n   either (EXT_KEY_DRIVER takes IY from $40B1 itself).\n2. $40A0 (the stack area's top, the string space's bottom) = the new top less 50, so the\n   string space is back to 50 bytes, as at power-on.\n3. IY = the new top: the workspace is IY+1 to IY+6, above the new top. Each call takes\n   another 6 bytes.\nWorkspace (the /64's): IY+1 the number of the key last taken ($FF: none), IY+2 the blink\ncounter, IY+3 a new key's number while it is checked, IY+4/IY+5 the repeat count (low,\nhigh), IY+6 the blink flag (0 blinks, $10 not). IY+0 is the top byte itself, which BASIC\nstill uses: a string was seen there.\nExit: IY = HL = the workspace's base.\nChanges: DE, HL, IY.\nObserved on the /64 (48K): $40B1 $FFFF -> $FFF9, $40A0 $FFCD -> $FFC7, $4049 and $401B =\n$FFF9; after A$=INKEY$ read CLEAR+SHIFT+B, IY+0 held $02.","comment":"HL = the top of memory"},
        "95": {"comment":"Less 6"},
        "99": {"comment":"The new top of memory"},
        "102": {"comment":"Kept at $4049 too"},
        "105": {"comment":"And in the keyboard block's last two bytes"},
        "108": {"comment":"Less 50"},
        "112": {"comment":"The stack area's top, 50 bytes of string space"},
        "115": {"comment":"IY = the workspace"},
        "120": {"synopsis":"The keyboard's driver while the extension is on (the keyboard block's driver at $4016, set\nby EXT_KEYB_SETUP; it replaces KEY_SCAN). With IY = the workspace (from the top of memory,\n$40B1) it reads a key (EXT_KEY_SCAN), then:\n1. No key (Z): returns A = 0.\n2. Code $01 (BREAK) with SHIFT: flips the blink flag (IY+6 bit 4), sends $0E (cursor on) to\n   the display and returns A = 0. BREAK alone is given as $01, with Z set.\n3. Codes $80 and up are given only when bit 0 of $4036 is set (nothing in the extension sets\n   it); otherwise A = 0 (EXT_KEY_DROP).\nUnlike KEY_SCAN it runs no RST $28 for BREAK.\nExit: A = the key's code, 0 for none; NZ for a code, except BREAK alone and the codes\nEXT_KEY_DECODE gives with Z.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.\nObserved: SHIFT+BREAK took IY+6 from 0 to $10 and back to 0; BREAK alone at the > prompt\nended the line, as with KEY_SCAN.","comment":"Keep the caller's IY"},
        "122": {"comment":"IY = the workspace, at the top of memory"},
        "126": {"comment":"Read a key"},
        "129": {"comment":"None?"},
        "131": {"comment":"BREAK ($01)?"},
        "135": {"comment":"SHIFT? E is $10 with it, 0 without"},
        "136": {"comment":"No: BREAK is given as it is"},
        "139": {"comment":"SHIFT+BREAK: flip the blink flag"},
        "147": {"comment":"$0E: the cursor on"},
        "152": {"comment":"No key"},
        "155": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DRIVER: a code to give; E + 1 is NZ for every code but BREAK alone (E $FF from the\ntest before).","comment":"NZ, but Z for BREAK alone"},
        "156": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DRIVER's end: IY back; with Z, A as it is; codes $80 and up only with bit 0 of $4036\nset.","comment":"The caller's IY back"},
        "158": {"comment":"Z: return A as it is"},
        "159": {"comment":"Below $80?"},
        "162": {"comment":"Bit 0 of $4036 lets $80 and up through"},
        "168": {"synopsis":"Returns A = 0 (no key), Z set: for a code EXT_KEY_DRIVER does not pass on, and for CLEAR\nwith SHIFT and a key that has no meaning there (from EXT_CLR_SYMBOL).","comment":"No key"},
        "170": {"synopsis":"Finds the key that is down now (the /64's own scan; it keeps no copy of the rows, so a held\nkey is found again on every pass):\n1. E = SHIFT ($3880 bit 0), C = row 6 ($3840).\n2. Rows 5 down to 0 ($3820 ... $3801): the first with any key down gives the number.\n3. None: row 6 alone; nothing there returns Z, A = 0. CLEAR ($3840 bit 1) with SHIFT returns\n   Z too; CLEAR alone, or other row-6 keys, give their number with C = $02 for CLEAR held,\n   else 0.\nThe key's number (EXT_KEY_NUMBER) is 8 x (row + 2) + bit + 1 for rows 0-5 and 9-16 for row\n6.\nExit: NZ with D = the number, E = $10 with SHIFT or 0, B = the row's bits, C = row 6's bits\n(or the CLEAR bit, for a row-6 key); Z and A = 0 for none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL.","comment":"Row 5 first"},
        "173": {"comment":"D = the row's number + 2"},
        "175": {"comment":"SHIFT, bit 0 of row 7"},
        "180": {"comment":"E = 1 with SHIFT"},
        "181": {"comment":"Row 6: ENTER, CLEAR, BREAK, arrows, SPACE"},
        "185": {"synopsis":"EXT_MATRIX_SCAN: one of rows 5 down to 0, L its address bit.","comment":"The row"},
        "187": {"comment":"A key down in it?"},
        "190": {"comment":"The next row down"},
        "191": {"comment":"L's bit to the next row; past row 0?"},
        "195": {"comment":"Only row 6 is left"},
        "197": {"comment":"Anything in it?"},
        "199": {"comment":"C = CLEAR held"},
        "202": {"comment":"No CLEAR: the row-6 key's number"},
        "204": {"comment":"CLEAR with SHIFT?"},
        "207": {"comment":"Then no key"},
        "208": {"synopsis":"EXT_MATRIX_SCAN: makes E $10 for SHIFT and D the key's number: 8 x D, then the place of the\nlowest bit set in B, from 1.\nEntry: D = the row's number + 2 (1 for row 6), B = the row's bits, E = SHIFT (1 or 0).\nExit: D = the number, NZ.","comment":"SHIFT"},
        "209": {"comment":"To bit 4: $10"},
        "214": {"comment":"A = 8 x D"},
        "215": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_NUMBER: 8 per pass, D passes.","comment":"8 more"},
        "217": {"comment":"D times"},
        "220": {"comment":"D = 8 x the row's number"},
        "221": {"comment":"The row's bits"},
        "222": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_NUMBER: one bit of the row per pass, until the set one is found.","comment":"The next bit into carry"},
        "223": {"comment":"Count it"},
        "224": {"comment":"The key's bit: done, NZ"},
        "225": {"comment":"Nothing"},
        "228": {"synopsis":"Turns a key's number into its code - the /64's own decoding, not KEY_SCAN's:\n- Row 6: ROW6_KEY_CODES, as KEY_SCAN takes them; given back at once.\n- Rows 0-3: $40 + n (n = row x 8 + bit): capitals, with SHIFT $60 + n, lower case.\n- Rows 4-5: $30-$3F with the last four keys moved to $2C-$2F; SHIFT flips bit 4. Then, with\n  no other row-6 key held, the code is given back. With CLEAR held (EXT_DEC_CLR_CHK) and\n  SHIFT: a letter gives its control code $01-$1A ($00 for @), \"!\" switches the special\n  characters on (bit 2 of $4036), '\"' off, \"*\" prints the screen (EXT_SCREEN_PRINT);\n  anything else gives no key.\nEntry: A = D = the key's number, E = $10 with SHIFT, C = row 6's bits (see EXT_MATRIX_SCAN).\nExit: A = the code. It is jumped to, so its RET returns from EXT_KEY_SCAN to $3081 in\nEXT_KEY_DRIVER (a control code goes on at EXT_KD_RETURN instead).\nObserved (A$=INKEY$ and ASC): CLEAR+SHIFT+B 2, CLEAR+SHIFT+Z 26, CLEAR+SHIFT+A 1, CLEAR\nalone 31, SHIFT+0 32, SHIFT+: 42, up 91, SHIFT+up 27; CLEAR+SHIFT+1 and +2 set and cleared\nbit 2 of $4036.","comment":"From 0"},
        "229": {"comment":"Row 6?"},
        "233": {"comment":"Rows 4-5?"},
        "237": {"comment":"Rows 0-3: $40 + n"},
        "239": {"comment":"With SHIFT $20 more: lower case"},
        "241": {"comment":"Check for CLEAR"},
        "243": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DECODE for row 6: the key's pair in ROW6_KEY_CODES ($0050), the second byte with\nSHIFT. Returns the code at once, NZ unless it is 0.","comment":"1-8: the bit + 1"},
        "245": {"comment":"Two bytes a key"},
        "246": {"comment":"SHIFT?"},
        "250": {"comment":"The shifted code"},
        "251": {"synopsis":"EXT_DEC_ROW6: reads the code from ROW6_KEY_CODES.","comment":"HL = $4E + 2 to $4E + 17"},
        "254": {"comment":"In ROW6_KEY_CODES at ROW6_KEY_CODES"},
        "258": {"comment":"The code"},
        "259": {"comment":"Z for none"},
        "261": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DECODE for rows 4-5 ($30-$3F): the last four keys go to $2C-$2F (, - . /), then\nSHIFT flips bit 4 (0 to space, 1 to !, : to *, , to < ...).","comment":"The last four keys of row 5?"},
        "265": {"comment":"$3C-$3F to $2C-$2F"},
        "267": {"synopsis":"EXT_DEC_DIGITS: SHIFT flips bit 4.","comment":"SHIFT flips bit 4"},
        "268": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DECODE: what row 6 holds as well. Nothing: the code is given back (NZ). CLEAR not\namong them: the code with Z. CLEAR: the CLEAR combinations.","comment":"Anything held in row 6?"},
        "271": {"comment":"CLEAR among it?"},
        "274": {"comment":"CLEAR+SHIFT and a letter?"},
        "278": {"comment":"Its control code, $00-$1F"},
        "280": {"comment":"Given past the BREAK test"},
        "282": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DECODE: CLEAR with a key that is not a shifted letter: \"*\" (SHIFT+:) prints the\nscreen, \"!\" (SHIFT+1) switches the special characters on (EXT_DISP_MAIN, bit 2 of $4036),\n'\"' (SHIFT+2) off. Anything else gives no key.\nExit: A = 0, Z.","comment":"CLEAR+\"*\"?"},
        "284": {"comment":"Print the screen"},
        "286": {"comment":"The display's flags at $4036"},
        "289": {"comment":"CLEAR+'\"'?"},
        "293": {"comment":"CLEAR+\"!\"?"},
        "295": {"comment":"Anything else: no key"},
        "298": {"comment":"Special characters on"},
        "300": {"comment":"No key"},
        "302": {"synopsis":"EXT_CLR_SYMBOL: '\"' switches the special characters off (bit 2 of $4036); no key.","comment":"Special characters off"},
        "304": {"comment":"No key"},
        "306": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DECODE: a control code from CLEAR+SHIFT+letter. Drops the return into EXT_KEY_DRIVER\n($3081) and goes on at EXT_KD_RETURN, so $01 (CLEAR+SHIFT+A) is not taken as BREAK; $00\n(CLEAR+SHIFT+@) returns with Z.","comment":"Drop the return to $3081"},
        "307": {"comment":"Past EXT_KEY_DRIVER's BREAK test"},
        "321": {"synopsis":"Prints the screen (CLEAR+SHIFT+: on the /64; SHIFT+down+P on Series I): when the printer is\nready (PRN_STATUS, Z), sends the 1024 bytes of $3C00-$3FFF to the printer (PRINTER_OUT), a\n$0D before each 64-byte row; a byte with bit 7 set (graphics) goes with bit 6 cleared. Then\nsix spaces, each followed by $0D. Printer not ready: nothing.\nThe code is Series I's, in two parts: here the status, from EXT_SPRINT_READY on the rest.\nExit: A = 0 (EXT_KEY_NONE), so the key gives no character.\nObserved: with Klive's printer not ready, CLEAR+SHIFT+: typed nothing.","comment":"Is the printer ready?"},
        "324": {"comment":"Past the display driver's entry"},
        "327": {"synopsis":"The display's driver while the extension is on (the display block's driver at $401E, set by\nEXT_HOOK_VECTORS from EXT_ON_DISPLAY): a jump to EXT_DISP_MAIN. It stays at Series I's\naddress, EXT_DISP_DRIVER, which EXT_ON_DISPLAY loads.","comment":"The driver itself"},
        "330": {"synopsis":"EXT_SCREEN_PRINT: printer not ready (NZ), no key (EXT_KEY_NONE). Else the screen from $3C00.","comment":"Not ready: no key"},
        "333": {"comment":"From the screen's start"},
        "336": {"synopsis":"EXT_SCREEN_PRINT: one screen byte; a $0D first at each row's start.","comment":"A row's start?"},
        "337": {"comment":"Low 6 bits 0?"},
        "339": {"comment":"A = $0D"},
        "341": {"comment":"Yes: a new line first"},
        "344": {"comment":"The screen byte"},
        "345": {"comment":"Graphics?"},
        "347": {"comment":"No"},
        "349": {"comment":"Yes: bit 6 off"},
        "351": {"synopsis":"EXT_SCREEN_PRINT: the end of the screen? Else print the byte and on.","comment":"The end of the screen?"},
        "353": {"comment":"Next byte"},
        "354": {"comment":"Yes: the end"},
        "359": {"comment":"On"},
        "361": {"synopsis":"EXT_SCREEN_PRINT: after the screen, six times a space and a $0D.","comment":"Six times"},
        "363": {"synopsis":"EXT_SPRINT_FEED: one space and $0D.","comment":"A space"},
        "368": {"comment":"A $0D"},
        "373": {"comment":"Again"},
        "375": {"comment":"No key"},
        "378": {"synopsis":"The 16 characters that may follow \"`\" for a special character, in code order: a e i o # $ u\n% & ) m ( g s b '. EXT_ACC_LOOKUP searches it (CPIR); the place found, 0-15, is the code for\na capital (or a digit), the place + $10 for lower case (or the symbol). With the /64's\ncharacter generator: A E I O 3 4 U 5 6 give the accented capitals Á É Í Ó Ö Ő Ú Ü Ű ($00-\n$08), 9 M 8 G S B 7 the block, λ, ∞, π, Σ, Ψ, Ω ($09-$0F); in lower case á ... ű ($10-$18)\nand √ μ α γ ∫ β ω ($19-$1F)."},
        "394": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_SCAN: a key is down (D its number). A new one (not IY+1): EXT_NEW_KEY. The same one:\nthe repeat count IY+4 (low) / IY+5 (high) goes down; until it runs out, no key. Then IY+4 =\n$60 and EXT_REPEAT_SET gives the key again, decoded afresh (SHIFT as it is now). The first\nrepeat comes after 5 x 256 passes, the next after 96 each.\nObserved: holding A for 100 frames typed 22 A's (Series I's extension: 13).","comment":"The key's number"},
        "395": {"comment":"The one last taken?"},
        "398": {"comment":"Three NOPs"},
        "401": {"comment":"No: a new key"},
        "404": {"comment":"Count the low byte down"},
        "407": {"comment":"Not yet: no key"},
        "409": {"comment":"Then the high byte"},
        "414": {"comment":"From now on 96 passes a repeat"},
        "418": {"comment":"The key again"},
        "422": {"synopsis":"The machine-code monitor, entered at $31A6 (SYSTEM, then /12710) or by a CALL. Takes the\nword on top of the stack as PC ($41FE), then MON_SAVE_REGS. Not reached from the rest of the\nROM. Entered through a CALL, the SP it shows still holds that return address. Observed on\nthe /64: SYSTEM /12710 showed the 12 registers, PC = 0000 (the word on the stack) and SP =\n4288.","comment":"HL = the word on the stack"},
        "423": {"comment":"Saved as PC"},
        "426": {"comment":"Back on the stack"},
        "427": {"synopsis":"Saves every register for the monitor and goes to its command loop:\nSP at $41FC, then IY, IX, AF, BC, DE, HL, AF', BC', DE', HL' as words up from $41E8 (pushed\ndown from $41FC; the register block MON_SHOW_REGS reads). That is BASIC's line buffer from\n$41E8 (its address at $40A7), so typing a line overwrites them. MON_BP_HIT comes here with\nPC already stored at $41FE.\nDoes not return.","comment":"Save SP"},
        "431": {"comment":"Push down from $41FC"},
        "434": {"comment":"The second set first"},
        "436": {"comment":"HL'"},
        "437": {"comment":"DE'"},
        "438": {"comment":"BC'"},
        "439": {"comment":"AF'"},
        "440": {"comment":"Back to the main set"},
        "442": {"comment":"HL"},
        "443": {"comment":"DE"},
        "444": {"comment":"BC"},
        "445": {"comment":"AF"},
        "446": {"comment":"IX"},
        "448": {"comment":"IY, at $41E8"},
        "450": {"comment":"The program's SP back"},
        "454": {"synopsis":"The monitor's command loop: clears the screen, shows the registers (MON_SHOW_REGS), waits\nfor a key (KEY_WAIT) and echoes it, then:\n- D: a memory dump (MON_DUMP);\n- M: changes memory (MON_MODIFY);\n- R: changes the saved registers (MON_EDIT_REGS);\n- B: back to BASIC (CLEAR_VARS, BASIC_READY);\n- G: runs from an address (MON_GO).\nAnything else shows the registers again.\nObserved on the /64: B went back to READY.","comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "457": {"comment":"Show the registers"},
        "460": {"comment":"Wait for a key"},
        "463": {"comment":"Echo it"},
        "466": {"comment":"D?"},
        "468": {"comment":"Yes: dump"},
        "470": {"comment":"M?"},
        "472": {"comment":"Yes: modify"},
        "474": {"comment":"R?"},
        "476": {"comment":"Yes: registers"},
        "478": {"comment":"B?"},
        "480": {"comment":"No"},
        "482": {"comment":"Clear the variables"},
        "485": {"comment":"To BASIC's prompt"},
        "488": {"synopsis":"MON_COMMAND: not B; G runs from an address (MON_GO), then the loop again.","comment":"G?"},
        "490": {"comment":"Yes: go"},
        "493": {"comment":"Show the registers again"},
        "495": {"synopsis":"D: reads an address (MON_READ_WORD; X goes back to MON_COMMAND) and shows 16 bytes from it\non a cleared screen: \"aaaa>\" then each byte as two hex digits and a space. Then a key: down\narrow ($0A) the next 16, up arrow ($5B) the 16 before, anything else MON_COMMAND. Observed\non the /64: D4000 showed 4000>C3 96 1C C3 D4 33 ..., RST $10's vector at $4003 being JP\nEXT_NEXT_CHAR.","comment":"HL = the address"},
        "498": {"comment":"X?"},
        "500": {"comment":"Yes: back"},
        "502": {"comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "505": {"synopsis":"MON_DUMP: one line of 16 bytes from HL.","comment":"The address"},
        "508": {"comment":"A = \">\""},
        "513": {"comment":"16 bytes"},
        "515": {"synopsis":"MON_DUMP_LINE: one byte and a space.","comment":"The byte"},
        "519": {"comment":"A space"},
        "524": {"comment":"Next byte"},
        "525": {"comment":"16 times"},
        "527": {"comment":"A = $0D"},
        "532": {"comment":"Wait for a key"},
        "535": {"comment":"Down arrow?"},
        "537": {"comment":"Yes: the next 16"},
        "539": {"comment":"Up arrow?"},
        "541": {"comment":"No: back to the commands"},
        "543": {"comment":"32 back: the 16 before"},
        "546": {"comment":"Carry clear"},
        "547": {"comment":"HL back"},
        "549": {"comment":"Show them"},
        "551": {"synopsis":"M: MON_MODIFY, then MON_COMMAND.","comment":"Change memory"},
        "554": {"comment":"Back to the commands"},
        "556": {"synopsis":"R: clears the screen, MON_EDIT_REGS, then MON_COMMAND.","comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "559": {"comment":"Change the registers"},
        "562": {"comment":"Back to the commands"},
        "564": {"synopsis":"Shows the 12 saved registers, one a line: the name from MON_REG_NAMES, \"=\" and the word from\nthe register block at $41E8 in hex (MON_REG_LINE).\nChanges: AF, BC, HL, IY.\nObserved on the /64: lines IY =4408 ... PC =0000; the second set's AF shows as \"AF =\", like\nthe first (MON_REG_NAMES).","comment":"IY = the register block"},
        "568": {"comment":"HL = the names"},
        "571": {"comment":"12 registers"},
        "573": {"synopsis":"MON_SHOW_REGS: one register.","comment":"Keep the count"},
        "574": {"comment":"Name and value"},
        "577": {"comment":"A = $0D"},
        "582": {"comment":"The count"},
        "583": {"comment":"IY to the next word"},
        "587": {"comment":"12 times"},
        "590": {"synopsis":"Prints a register's 3-character name from HL, \"=\" and the word at IY (high byte first) in\nhex.\nEntry: HL = the name, IY = the saved word.\nExit: HL past the name.\nChanges: AF, B, C, HL.","comment":"3 characters"},
        "592": {"synopsis":"MON_REG_LINE: one character of the name.","comment":"A name's character"},
        "597": {"comment":"3 times"},
        "599": {"comment":"A = \"=\""},
        "604": {"comment":"The high byte"},
        "610": {"comment":"The low byte"},
        "617": {"synopsis":"Prints HL as four hex digits (MON_HEX_A twice).\nChanges: AF, C.","comment":"H"},
        "621": {"comment":"L"},
        "626": {"synopsis":"Changes memory: reads an address (MON_READ_WORD; X returns), then for each byte on a cleared\nscreen \"aaaa>\", the byte, \"-\", and two hex digits typed (MON_READ_BYTE) that replace it;\nthen the next address. X while typing a byte ends it (MON_READ_NIBBLE makes MON_MODIFY\nreturn).","comment":"HL = the address"},
        "629": {"comment":"X?"},
        "631": {"comment":"Yes: done"},
        "632": {"comment":"Clear the screen"},
        "635": {"synopsis":"MON_MODIFY: one byte at HL.","comment":"The address"},
        "638": {"comment":"A = \">\""},
        "643": {"comment":"The byte there"},
        "647": {"comment":"A = \"-\""},
        "652": {"comment":"B = the new byte"},
        "655": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "656": {"comment":"A = $0D"},
        "661": {"comment":"Next address"},
        "662": {"comment":"And on"},
        "664": {"synopsis":"The register names the monitor prints, three characters each in the order of the register\nblock at $41E8: \"IY \", \"IX \", \"AF \", \"BC \", \"DE \", \"HL \", \"AF\" and $19, \"BC'\", \"DE'\", \"HL'\",\n\"SP \", \"PC \", and a zero. MON_REG_LINE reads them three at a time; the zero is not read.\nThe second AF's third byte is $19 where the Series I extension has \"'\" ($27); the display\ntakes $19 as a control code (cursor right, DISP_CONTROL), so that name shows as \"AF \" with a\ngap. The other three names keep \"'\".\nObserved: the seventh line reads AF =FFFF, its third screen byte a space."},
        "701": {"synopsis":"R: for each of the 12 saved registers prints its name and value (MON_REG_LINE) and \"/\", then\nreads four hex digits (MON_READ_WORD) as its new value; X keeps the old one. SP and PC are\namong them.\nChanges: AF, BC, IY.","comment":"HL = the names"},
        "704": {"comment":"IY = the register block"},
        "708": {"comment":"12 registers"},
        "710": {"synopsis":"MON_EDIT_REGS: one register.","comment":"Keep the count"},
        "711": {"comment":"Name and value"},
        "714": {"comment":"A = \"/\""},
        "719": {"comment":"Keep the names' place"},
        "720": {"comment":"HL = the new value"},
        "723": {"comment":"X?"},
        "725": {"comment":"Yes: keep the old one"},
        "727": {"comment":"Store the new value"},
        "733": {"synopsis":"MON_EDIT_REGS: on to the next word of the block.","comment":"IY to the next word"},
        "737": {"comment":"The names' place"},
        "738": {"comment":"A = $0D"},
        "743": {"comment":"The count"},
        "744": {"comment":"12 times"},
        "747": {"synopsis":"G: reads an address (two MON_READ_BYTEs; X returns) as the new PC ($41FE), then waits for a\nkey:\n- NEW LINE: runs from PC (MON_RESUME);\n- \",\": reads a breakpoint's address (X returns), keeps its 3 bytes at $4200-$4202, writes\n  CALL MON_BP_HIT there and runs (MON_RESUME);\n- X: returns, PC already changed;\n- anything else is ignored.\nOne breakpoint at a time: a second one would overwrite the bytes kept for the first.","comment":"H = the high byte"},
        "751": {"comment":"L = the low byte"},
        "755": {"comment":"The new PC"},
        "758": {"synopsis":"MON_GO: waits for NEW LINE, \",\" or X.","comment":"Wait for a key"},
        "761": {"comment":"\",\"?"},
        "763": {"comment":"Yes: a breakpoint"},
        "765": {"comment":"NEW LINE?"},
        "767": {"comment":"Yes: run"},
        "769": {"comment":"X?"},
        "771": {"comment":"Yes: done"},
        "772": {"comment":"Else wait again"},
        "774": {"synopsis":"MON_GO's \",\": the breakpoint's address, its bytes kept at $4200, CALL MON_BP_HIT ($CD $47\n$33) written over them.","comment":"Echo the \",\""},
        "777": {"comment":"HL = the breakpoint's address"},
        "780": {"comment":"X?"},
        "782": {"comment":"Yes: done"},
        "783": {"comment":"Keep the address"},
        "784": {"comment":"Its 3 bytes to $4200"},
        "790": {"comment":"Kept"},
        "792": {"comment":"The address"},
        "793": {"comment":"DE = MON_BP_HIT"},
        "796": {"comment":"A = CALL"},
        "798": {"comment":"Written over the 3 bytes"},
        "800": {"comment":"Its target, low"},
        "802": {"comment":"And high"},
        "803": {"synopsis":"Runs the program with the saved registers: pushes PC ($41FE) on the saved stack ($41FC),\npops IY, IX, AF, BC, DE, HL and the second set from $41E8 on, sets SP and returns to PC.\nDoes not return.","comment":"The program's stack"},
        "807": {"comment":"HL = PC"},
        "810": {"comment":"Pushed, for the RET"},
        "811": {"comment":"The stack with it"},
        "815": {"comment":"Pop up from the register block"},
        "818": {"comment":"IY"},
        "820": {"comment":"IX"},
        "822": {"comment":"AF"},
        "823": {"comment":"BC"},
        "824": {"comment":"DE"},
        "825": {"comment":"HL"},
        "826": {"comment":"The second set"},
        "828": {"comment":"AF'"},
        "829": {"comment":"BC'"},
        "830": {"comment":"DE'"},
        "831": {"comment":"HL'"},
        "832": {"comment":"Back to the main set"},
        "834": {"comment":"The program's SP"},
        "838": {"comment":"Run from PC"},
        "839": {"synopsis":"Where a breakpoint's CALL lands: PC ($41FE) = the CALL's address (its return address - 3),\nthe 3 kept bytes ($4200) go back over it, and with HL restored it goes on at MON_SAVE_REGS\nwith the stack as the program had it. AF, BC and DE are kept on the stack around the LDIR,\nso on the /64 every register shows as the program had it (Series I kept A, B and DE at\n$4203-$4206 and lost C and F). The breakpoint is gone once it is hit.","comment":"HL = the CALL's return, its HL on the stack"},
        "840": {"comment":"Back 3: the breakpoint's address"},
        "843": {"comment":"Keep AF, BC, DE for the LDIR"},
        "846": {"comment":"Saved as PC"},
        "849": {"comment":"DE = the kept bytes"},
        "852": {"comment":"HL = them, DE = the breakpoint"},
        "853": {"comment":"3 bytes"},
        "856": {"comment":"Put them back"},
        "858": {"comment":"DE, BC, AF back"},
        "861": {"comment":"The return: MON_SAVE_REGS"},
        "864": {"comment":"HL back, the return on the stack"},
        "865": {"comment":"To MON_SAVE_REGS"},
        "866": {"synopsis":"EXT_ACC_LETTER: the character after \"`\" is not a letter. A digit or $3A-$3F (bit 4 of C)\ncounts as a capital (bit 5 of C cleared); then A's bit 4 is cleared, so 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 look\nfor # $ % & ' ( ) in EXT_ACCENT_KEYS.","comment":"A digit ($30-$3F)?"},
        "870": {"comment":"Then it counts as a capital"},
        "872": {"synopsis":"EXT_ACC_DIGIT: $30-$3F to $20-$2F.","comment":"Digits to the symbols above them"},
        "874": {"synopsis":"EXT_ACC_LETTER: keeps the character (C) and goes to EXT_ACC_LOOKUP.","comment":"Keep the character"},
        "883": {"synopsis":"Prints A as two hex digits (MON_HEX_DIGIT).\nChanges: AF, C.","comment":"Keep the byte"},
        "884": {"comment":"The high nibble"},
        "892": {"comment":"As a hex digit"},
        "895": {"comment":"The byte"},
        "896": {"comment":"The low nibble"},
        "898": {"comment":"As a hex digit"},
        "902": {"synopsis":"Prints the value 0-15 in A as a hex digit, 0-9 or A-F (DISPLAY_CHAR).\nChanges: AF.","comment":"To \"0\"-\"9\""},
        "904": {"comment":"Above \"9\"?"},
        "906": {"comment":"No"},
        "908": {"comment":"Yes: \"A\"-\"F\""},
        "910": {"synopsis":"MON_HEX_DIGIT: prints the digit."},
        "914": {"synopsis":"Reads four hex digits from the keyboard, echoing them, into HL (MON_READ_BYTE twice).\nExit: HL = the word; A = the last digit typed, or \"X\" if X was pressed (HL kept).\nChanges: AF, B, C, HL.","comment":"The high byte"},
        "917": {"comment":"H"},
        "918": {"comment":"The low byte"},
        "921": {"comment":"L"},
        "923": {"synopsis":"Reads two hex digits from the keyboard (MON_READ_NIBBLE), echoing each, into B. X makes the\nroutine that called MON_READ_BYTE return, with A = \"X\".\nExit: B = the byte, A = the second digit's character.\nChanges: AF, B, C.","comment":"The first digit"},
        "926": {"comment":"Times 16"},
        "934": {"comment":"B = the high nibble"},
        "935": {"comment":"The character"},
        "936": {"comment":"Echo it"},
        "939": {"comment":"The second digit"},
        "942": {"comment":"Added"},
        "943": {"comment":"B = the byte"},
        "944": {"comment":"The character"},
        "945": {"comment":"Echo it"},
        "949": {"synopsis":"Waits (KEY_WAIT) for a hex digit, 0-9 or A-F; other keys are ignored. X drops its own return\naddress and MON_READ_BYTE's, so the routine that called MON_READ_BYTE returns with A = \"X\"\n(HL kept): that is how every monitor input is cancelled.\nExit: A = the digit's value, C = its character.\nChanges: AF, C.","comment":"Wait for a key"},
        "952": {"comment":"X?"},
        "954": {"comment":"No"},
        "956": {"comment":"Drop our return, HL back on the stack"},
        "957": {"comment":"HL, SP at MON_READ_BYTE's return"},
        "958": {"comment":"Drop it too, HL back"},
        "959": {"comment":"HL"},
        "960": {"comment":"Return from MON_READ_BYTE's caller"},
        "961": {"synopsis":"MON_READ_NIBBLE: is the key a hex digit?","comment":"C = the character"},
        "962": {"comment":"Below \"0\"?"},
        "964": {"comment":"Yes: ignored"},
        "966": {"comment":"\"0\"-\"9\": the value"},
        "968": {"comment":"Yes"},
        "969": {"comment":"Below \"A\"?"},
        "971": {"comment":"Yes: ignored"},
        "973": {"comment":"$0A-$0F for \"A\"-\"F\""},
        "975": {"comment":"\"A\"-\"F\"?"},
        "977": {"comment":"Yes"},
        "978": {"comment":"Else ignored"},
        "980": {"synopsis":"RST $10's handler while the extension is on (the RAM vector at $4004, set by\nEXT_HOOK_VECTORS). From anywhere but EXEC_STATEMENT's first read (return address $1D5B) it\nis NEXT_CHAR. There, it reads the statement's first character (NEXT_CHAR); \"R\" with \"E\"\nright after it is the renumber command (RENUM_CMD), anything else is given back as NEXT_CHAR\ngave it.\nExit: as NEXT_CHAR.\nSince BASIC has no keyword RE, a statement starting with the letters RE (an assignment to RE\nor REX, say, or RENUM) is taken as the command.\nObserved on Series I (the same code): RENUM stops with SN.","comment":"HL = the caller's return address"},
        "981": {"comment":"High byte $1D?"},
        "984": {"comment":"No"},
        "986": {"comment":"Low byte $5B?"},
        "989": {"synopsis":"EXT_NEXT_CHAR: from elsewhere, plain NEXT_CHAR.","comment":"Text pointer back, return on the stack"},
        "990": {"comment":"Not EXEC_STATEMENT's read: NEXT_CHAR"},
        "993": {"comment":"The statement's first character"},
        "996": {"comment":"Keep its flags"},
        "997": {"comment":"And its place"},
        "998": {"comment":"\"R\"?"},
        "1000": {"comment":"No"},
        "1002": {"comment":"The next byte"},
        "1003": {"comment":"As it is in the text"},
        "1004": {"comment":"\"E\"?"},
        "1006": {"comment":"Yes: the renumber command"},
        "1009": {"synopsis":"EXT_NEXT_CHAR: not RE; NEXT_CHAR's results back to EXEC_STATEMENT.","comment":"Not RE"},
        "1010": {"comment":"NEXT_CHAR's results"},
        "1012": {"synopsis":"Writes HL as decimal digits without leading zeros: PUT_INT_DIGITS writes five digits\n(through $4121) into the line buffer (its address at $40A7), then up to four leading zeros\nare passed over.\nEntry: HL = the number.\nExit: HL = the first digit, B = how many (1-5); DE kept; carry clear.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.","comment":"Keep DE"},
        "1013": {"comment":"The number for PUT_INT_DIGITS"},
        "1016": {"comment":"No punctuation"},
        "1019": {"comment":"HL = the line buffer"},
        "1022": {"comment":"Keep its start"},
        "1023": {"comment":"Five digits there"},
        "1026": {"comment":"The first digit"},
        "1027": {"comment":"Five at most"},
        "1029": {"synopsis":"RENUM_NUM_TEXT: passes over a leading 0, five at most.","comment":"The digit"},
        "1030": {"comment":"A 0?"},
        "1032": {"comment":"No: the first digit found"},
        "1034": {"comment":"Pass over it"},
        "1035": {"comment":"Up to five"},
        "1037": {"comment":"All zero: the last \"0\""},
        "1038": {"comment":"One digit"},
        "1039": {"synopsis":"RENUM_NUM_TEXT: the digits found.","comment":"DE back"},
        "1041": {"synopsis":"Steps IY to the next 4-byte entry of the renumber table.","comment":"Next entry: 4 bytes on"},
        "1050": {"synopsis":"Finds the next GOTO ($8D), GOSUB ($91), THEN ($CA) or ELSE ($95) token in a line's text.\nEntry: HL = the byte before the place to search from.\nExit: NZ and HL at the token; or Z and HL at the line's closing 0.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"Next byte"},
        "1052": {"comment":"The line's end?"},
        "1053": {"comment":"Yes: Z"},
        "1054": {"comment":"GOTO?"},
        "1058": {"comment":"GOSUB?"},
        "1062": {"comment":"THEN?"},
        "1066": {"comment":"ELSE?"},
        "1068": {"comment":"No: on"},
        "1070": {"synopsis":"RENUM_FIND_REF: a token found; NZ for it.","comment":"NZ: a token"},
        "1072": {"synopsis":"Copies the decimal digits at HL, passing over spaces, into the line buffer (its address at\n$40A7) with a closing 0.\nEntry: HL = the text after the token.\nExit: B = the number of digits, Z when there is none; DE = the line buffer; HL at the first\nbyte that is not a digit or a space.\nChanges: AF, B, HL.","comment":"DE = the line buffer"},
        "1076": {"comment":"Kept for the exit"},
        "1077": {"comment":"No digits yet"},
        "1079": {"synopsis":"RENUM_COPY_NUM: one byte of the text.","comment":"The text's byte"},
        "1080": {"comment":"A space?"},
        "1082": {"comment":"Yes: passed over"},
        "1084": {"comment":"Below \"0\"?"},
        "1086": {"comment":"Yes: the end"},
        "1088": {"comment":"Above \"9\"?"},
        "1090": {"comment":"Yes: the end"},
        "1092": {"comment":"Count the digit"},
        "1093": {"comment":"Copy it"},
        "1095": {"synopsis":"RENUM_COPY_NUM: on to the next byte.","comment":"Next byte"},
        "1096": {"comment":"On"},
        "1098": {"synopsis":"RENUM_COPY_NUM: closes the copy with a 0; Z if no digit.","comment":"A closing 0"},
        "1100": {"comment":"DE = the line buffer"},
        "1101": {"comment":"Z if no digits"},
        "1104": {"synopsis":"Puts a new number's digits in place of an old one's in the program text, making room or\nclosing up first (RENUM_INSERT, RENUM_REMOVE) when the counts differ.\nEntry: HL = the old digits in the text, C = their count, DE = the new digits, B = their\ncount; carry clear (the compare's first SBC relies on it).\nExit: HL past the new digits.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL.","comment":"Keep the counts"},
        "1105": {"synopsis":"RENUM_FIT_NUM: counts B and C down together; the one that reaches 0 first is the shorter.","comment":"New count less the old one"},
        "1107": {"comment":"The same?"},
        "1109": {"comment":"Count the new one down"},
        "1110": {"comment":"Zero first: fewer new digits"},
        "1112": {"comment":"Count the old one down"},
        "1113": {"comment":"Not zero yet"},
        "1115": {"comment":"More new digits: make room for B"},
        "1118": {"comment":"Then copy"},
        "1120": {"synopsis":"RENUM_FIT_NUM: fewer new digits; B = the difference, RENUM_REMOVE.","comment":"B = the difference"},
        "1122": {"comment":"Close up B bytes"},
        "1125": {"synopsis":"RENUM_FIT_NUM: copies the B new digits from DE to HL.","comment":"B = the new count"},
        "1126": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PUT_DIGITS: one digit.","comment":"A new digit"},
        "1127": {"comment":"Into the text"},
        "1130": {"comment":"B times"},
        "1133": {"synopsis":"The renumber command, RE [first][,step] (an extension command; BASIC has none). Both default\nto 10 ($40E2 and $40E4, the AUTO statement's number and step). The first number is read only\nwhen it comes straight after RE or after spaces; \",step\" must follow RE at once. A 0 or a\nmissing number where one is expected is error SN (SYNTAX_ERROR).\n1. RENUM_PASS1: a table of every line number after GOTO, GOSUB, THEN and ELSE.\n2. RENUM_P2_LINE: each table entry of an old number gets that line's new number.\n3. RENUM_PASS3: each line's own number, and every number after those tokens, rewritten.\n4. LINE_STORED (at $2C83): relinks the program, clears the variables, COMMAND_LOOP.\nEntry: HL at the \"E\"; AF and HL of EXT_NEXT_CHAR left on the stack (BASIC's stack is reset\nat the end).\nNumbers after other statements (RUN, LIST, ...) are not changed; a reference to a line that\ndoes not exist keeps its number.\nOn the /64 a reference that gets shorter by more than one digit is damaged (RENUM_REMOVE).\nObserved on the /64: RE 100,5 made lines 10, 20, 30 into 100, 105, 110 with GOTO, GOSUB, ON\nGOTO lists, THEN and ELSE following, and RE alone made them 10, 20, 30 again; RE 1,1 on 10\nGOTO 1000 / 20 / 1000 gave 1 GOTO 300.","comment":"Past the \"E\""},
        "1134": {"comment":"Default 10"},
        "1137": {"comment":"For the first number"},
        "1141": {"comment":"And the step"},
        "1145": {"comment":"What follows RE"},
        "1146": {"comment":"The end of the line?"},
        "1147": {"comment":"Yes: the defaults"},
        "1149": {"comment":"A comma?"},
        "1151": {"comment":"Yes: the step only"},
        "1153": {"comment":"DE = the first number"},
        "1156": {"comment":"Keep the character after it"},
        "1157": {"comment":"DE = 0?"},
        "1159": {"comment":"Yes: SN"},
        "1161": {"comment":"The character after it"},
        "1162": {"comment":"The first number"},
        "1166": {"comment":"A comma?"},
        "1168": {"comment":"No: no step"},
        "1170": {"synopsis":"RENUM_CMD: \",step\": reads the step into $40E4.","comment":"Past the comma"},
        "1171": {"comment":"DE = the step"},
        "1174": {"comment":"The step"},
        "1178": {"comment":"DE = 0?"},
        "1180": {"synopsis":"RENUM_CMD: a number that is 0 (or missing) is error SN.","comment":"Yes: SN"},
        "1183": {"synopsis":"Builds the renumber table, 256 bytes past the program's end ($40F9): for every line number\nafter GOTO, GOSUB, THEN or ELSE (and each one of a list after them, \"ON A GOTO 10,20\"), a\n4-byte entry: the number of its digits, the number (low, high) and a done flag (0). An $FF\nends the table. Each entry checks the room against the top of memory ($40B1) only: OM_ERROR\nwhen it does not fit.","comment":"IY = the program's end"},
        "1187": {"comment":"256 on"},
        "1190": {"comment":"The table's start"},
        "1192": {"comment":"Kept for pass 2"},
        "1194": {"comment":"HL = the program's start"},
        "1197": {"comment":"Kept for pass 2"},
        "1198": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS1: the next line; a link of 0 ends the program (RENUM_P1_END).","comment":"The line's link"},
        "1199": {"comment":"Its high byte"},
        "1200": {"comment":"0: the program's end?"},
        "1201": {"comment":"Yes"},
        "1204": {"comment":"To the line number"},
        "1205": {"comment":"To its high byte"},
        "1206": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS1: the next reference token in the line (RENUM_FIND_REF); at the line's end on to\nthe next line.","comment":"The next reference token"},
        "1209": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "1210": {"comment":"The line's end: the next line"},
        "1212": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS1: after a token, the digits (RENUM_COPY_NUM); none, back to the scan. Else the\nroom check and an entry for the number.","comment":"The digits after it"},
        "1215": {"comment":"Back one"},
        "1216": {"comment":"None: on with the scan"},
        "1218": {"comment":"Past the last digit again"},
        "1219": {"comment":"Keep the text's place"},
        "1220": {"comment":"Keep the line buffer"},
        "1221": {"comment":"DE = the table's place"},
        "1224": {"comment":"The top of memory"},
        "1227": {"comment":"Below the table?"},
        "1229": {"comment":"Yes: OM"},
        "1232": {"comment":"4 bytes for an entry"},
        "1235": {"comment":"Room for it?"},
        "1237": {"comment":"No: OM"},
        "1240": {"comment":"The digit count"},
        "1243": {"comment":"HL = the line buffer"},
        "1244": {"comment":"DE = the number"},
        "1247": {"comment":"Low byte"},
        "1250": {"comment":"High byte"},
        "1253": {"comment":"Not done yet"},
        "1257": {"comment":"Next entry"},
        "1260": {"comment":"The text's place"},
        "1261": {"comment":"Back one"},
        "1262": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS1: passes over spaces after the number; a comma means another number.","comment":"Next byte"},
        "1264": {"comment":"A space?"},
        "1266": {"comment":"Yes: passed over"},
        "1268": {"comment":"A comma?"},
        "1270": {"comment":"Yes: another number"},
        "1272": {"comment":"Back one for the scan"},
        "1273": {"comment":"On with the scan"},
        "1275": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS1: a comma; the next number of the list.","comment":"Past the comma"},
        "1276": {"comment":"The next number"},
        "1278": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS1: the program's end; $FF closes the table, HL = the program's start, IY = the\ntable, DE = the first new number, then pass 2.","comment":"$FF ends the table"},
        "1282": {"comment":"HL = the program's start"},
        "1283": {"comment":"IY = the table"},
        "1285": {"comment":"DE = the first new number"},
        "1289": {"comment":"Kept for pass 3"},
        "1290": {"comment":"The table, kept"},
        "1292": {"comment":"The program's start, kept"},
        "1293": {"synopsis":"Pass 2, one line: its link and old number (BCDE_FROM_MEM; link 0 ends the pass at\nRENUM_PASS3), then every table entry not yet done that holds the old number gets the new one\n(DE) and is marked done. The next new number is DE + step; past 65528 (or a carry) is\nOM_ERROR, even for the number after the last line.","comment":"The new number"},
        "1294": {"comment":"DE = the link, BC = the old number"},
        "1297": {"comment":"Link 0?"},
        "1299": {"comment":"Yes: pass 3"},
        "1301": {"comment":"HL = the next line"},
        "1302": {"comment":"DE = the new number"},
        "1303": {"comment":"The table's start"},
        "1305": {"synopsis":"RENUM_P2_LINE: one table entry; $FF ends the table.","comment":"The entry's first byte"},
        "1308": {"comment":"$FF: the table's end?"},
        "1311": {"comment":"The done flag"},
        "1314": {"comment":"Done already?"},
        "1317": {"comment":"The number's low byte"},
        "1320": {"comment":"The old number's?"},
        "1323": {"comment":"High byte"},
        "1326": {"comment":"The old number's?"},
        "1329": {"comment":"Yes: the new number"},
        "1335": {"comment":"Done"},
        "1339": {"synopsis":"RENUM_P2_LINE: the next entry.","comment":"Next entry"},
        "1342": {"comment":"On"},
        "1344": {"synopsis":"RENUM_P2_LINE: the table done; DE = the next new number, checked.","comment":"The table's start back"},
        "1346": {"comment":"Keep the next line"},
        "1347": {"comment":"HL = the step"},
        "1350": {"comment":"Plus the new number"},
        "1351": {"comment":"Past 65535: OM"},
        "1354": {"comment":"DE = the next new number"},
        "1355": {"comment":"65528"},
        "1358": {"comment":"Above it?"},
        "1360": {"comment":"Yes: OM"},
        "1363": {"comment":"The next line"},
        "1364": {"comment":"On"},
        "1366": {"synopsis":"Pass 3: from the program's start, each line gets its new number (from the first one, in\nsteps), and the numbers after the reference tokens are rewritten from the table in the order\npass 1 found them (RENUM_P3_REF). Ends at LINE_STORED ($2C83).","comment":"Drop the new number"},
        "1367": {"comment":"HL = the program's start"},
        "1368": {"comment":"IY = the table"},
        "1370": {"comment":"DE = the first new number"},
        "1371": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS3: one line; a link of 0 ends the program. Writes the line's new number.","comment":"The line's link"},
        "1372": {"comment":"Its high byte"},
        "1373": {"comment":"0: the program's end?"},
        "1374": {"comment":"Yes: relink and back to BASIC"},
        "1377": {"comment":"To the line number"},
        "1378": {"comment":"The new number, low"},
        "1380": {"comment":"And high"},
        "1381": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS3: the next reference token in the line; at the line's end the next line with DE +\nstep.","comment":"The next reference token"},
        "1384": {"comment":"Past it"},
        "1385": {"comment":"Found one?"},
        "1387": {"comment":"The line's end: keep it"},
        "1388": {"comment":"HL = the step"},
        "1391": {"comment":"Plus the new number"},
        "1392": {"comment":"DE = the next new number"},
        "1393": {"comment":"HL = the next line"},
        "1394": {"comment":"On"},
        "1396": {"synopsis":"RENUM_PASS3: after a token with digits (RENUM_COPY_NUM), the next table entry's number as\ntext (RENUM_NUM_TEXT) goes in place of the old digits (RENUM_FIT_NUM); the entry's first\nbyte gives the old digit count.","comment":"Keep the place"},
        "1397": {"comment":"Keep the new number"},
        "1398": {"comment":"The digits after it"},
        "1401": {"comment":"The new number back"},
        "1402": {"comment":"The place"},
        "1403": {"comment":"Back one"},
        "1404": {"comment":"None: on with the scan"},
        "1406": {"synopsis":"RENUM_P3_REF: passes over the spaces before the digits.","comment":"Next byte"},
        "1408": {"comment":"A space?"},
        "1410": {"comment":"Yes: passed over"},
        "1412": {"comment":"Keep the new number"},
        "1413": {"comment":"Keep the old digits' place"},
        "1414": {"comment":"HL = the entry's new number"},
        "1420": {"comment":"As text"},
        "1423": {"comment":"C = the old digit count"},
        "1426": {"comment":"Next entry"},
        "1429": {"comment":"DE = the new digits"},
        "1430": {"comment":"HL = the old digits"},
        "1431": {"comment":"In place of the old"},
        "1434": {"comment":"The new number back"},
        "1435": {"comment":"Back one"},
        "1436": {"synopsis":"RENUM_P3_REF: passes over spaces after the new digits; a comma means another number.","comment":"Next byte"},
        "1438": {"comment":"A space?"},
        "1440": {"comment":"Yes: passed over"},
        "1442": {"comment":"A comma?"},
        "1444": {"comment":"Yes: another number"},
        "1446": {"comment":"Back one for the scan"},
        "1447": {"comment":"On with the scan"},
        "1449": {"synopsis":"RENUM_P3_REF: a comma; the next number of the list.","comment":"Past the comma"},
        "1450": {"comment":"The next number"},
        "1452": {"synopsis":"Closes up B bytes at HL - on Series I. On the /64 the byte at $05B9 is $00 where Series I\nhas $10 (DJNZ back to RENUM_REM_LOOP), so the bytes after it no longer decode as Series I's\nLD ($40F9),HL but as CALL M,$F922 and LD B,B:\n- the loop runs once, so only one byte is closed up, whatever B is;\n- the program's end ($40F9) is not lowered;\n- the CALL M is not taken: S is clear from RENUM_SHRINK's DEC B (a difference of 1-4). A\n  reference one digit shorter comes out right but for $40F9; two or more digits shorter\n  leaves old digits in the text. The image matches the\nchecksums of the MAME ht108064 set (ht1080z-roms-readme.txt): the dump holds this byte.\nEntry: HL = the place, B = how many.\nChanges: AF (BC, DE, HL kept).\nObserved: RE 1,1 on 10 GOTO 1000 / 20 PRINT 5 / 1000 END listed 1 GOTO 300 and $40F9 stayed\n$4304.","comment":"Keep the registers"},
        "1455": {"comment":"DE = the place"},
        "1457": {"comment":"Kept twice"},
        "1459": {"comment":"The program's end"},
        "1462": {"comment":"Kept"},
        "1463": {"synopsis":"RENUM_REMOVE: the new end and the source; on the /64 one pass only (see there).","comment":"The end down"},
        "1464": {"comment":"The source up"},
        "1465": {"comment":"$00 here: Series I has DJNZ, the loop is gone"},
        "1466": {"comment":"LD ($40F9),HL's bytes, read as CALL M; S is clear"},
        "1469": {"comment":"The rest of that LD; the new end is not stored"},
        "1470": {"comment":"The old end"},
        "1471": {"comment":"BC = the place"},
        "1472": {"comment":"Bytes from the place to the old end"},
        "1475": {"comment":"As the count"},
        "1477": {"comment":"HL = the place"},
        "1478": {"comment":"HL = place + B, DE = the place"},
        "1479": {"comment":"Move them down"},
        "1481": {"comment":"The registers back"},
        "1485": {"synopsis":"Makes room for B bytes at HL: the program's end ($40F9) goes up by B and the bytes from HL\non move up by B (LDDR from the old end). No check that the memory is there.\nEntry: HL = the place, B = how many.\nChanges: AF (BC, DE, HL kept).","comment":"Keep the registers"},
        "1488": {"comment":"The program's end"},
        "1491": {"comment":"DE = the old end"},
        "1493": {"synopsis":"RENUM_INSERT: the new end, a byte per pass.","comment":"The end up"},
        "1494": {"comment":"B times"},
        "1496": {"comment":"The new end"},
        "1499": {"comment":"BC = the place"},
        "1501": {"comment":"Keep the new end"},
        "1502": {"comment":"Carry clear"},
        "1503": {"comment":"Bytes from the place to the new end"},
        "1505": {"comment":"As the count"},
        "1507": {"comment":"With the last"},
        "1508": {"comment":"HL = the new end"},
        "1509": {"comment":"HL = the old end, DE = the new end"},
        "1510": {"comment":"Move them up"},
        "1512": {"comment":"The registers back"},
        "1516": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DOWN: on to EXT_REPEAT_SET."},
        "1522": {"synopsis":"Returns A = 0 (no key), Z set.","comment":"No key"},
        "1524": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DOWN found a key that is not the last one: sets the repeat count for the first\nrepeat (IY+4 = 0, IY+5 = 5: 5 x 256 passes), waits DELAY_BC $0400 and scans again\n(EXT_MATRIX_SCAN). Gone, or another key: no key (EXT_KEY_NONE). The same: recorded as the\nkey last taken (EXT_KEY_RECORD) and decoded.\nEntry: D = the key's number.","comment":"The first repeat's count: 5 x 256"},
        "1532": {"comment":"Wait for the contacts to settle"},
        "1538": {"comment":"Keep the key's number"},
        "1541": {"comment":"Scan again"},
        "1544": {"comment":"Gone: no key"},
        "1546": {"comment":"Still the same key?"},
        "1550": {"comment":"No: no key"},
        "1552": {"comment":"Take it"},
        "1555": {"synopsis":"Reads the keyboard for EXT_KEY_DRIVER, with auto-repeat and a blinking cursor:\n1. While the cursor is shown ($4022, the display block's kept character, not 0) and the\n   blink flag (IY+6) is 0: counts IY+2 down; each time it runs out $4037 goes down, and on\n   every second time IY+2 = $90, $4037 = 1 and the screen byte at the cursor ($4020) is\n   flipped with $7F: the cursor $5F becomes a space and back.\n2. The key down now (EXT_MATRIX_SCAN): one, EXT_KEY_DOWN. None: IY+1 and IY+3 = $FF (no key\n   last taken), IY+4 = 3, IY+5 = 1, and A = 0, Z.\n$4036 and $4037 are KEY_SCAN's copies of rows 0 and 1, which the extension uses for its own\nflags (it keeps no copies).\nEntry: IY = the workspace.\nExit: A = the code and NZ, or A = 0 and Z (but see EXT_KEY_DECODE).\nObserved: the screen byte at the cursor alternated $5F and $20, about 11 frames each, at the\nprompt.","comment":"The cursor shown?"},
        "1561": {"comment":"Blinking on?"},
        "1567": {"comment":"Count down"},
        "1572": {"comment":"And the second count"},
        "1576": {"comment":"Not past 0 yet"},
        "1579": {"comment":"The counts for the next flip"},
        "1585": {"comment":"The cursor's address"},
        "1590": {"comment":"$5F and space flipped"},
        "1593": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_SCAN: the scan, after the blink.","comment":"The key down now"},
        "1596": {"comment":"One: EXT_KEY_DOWN"},
        "1599": {"comment":"None: forget the key"},
        "1612": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_MAIN, special characters on, no \"`\" pending: a \"`\" ($60) is held back - bit 3 of\n$4036 set, nothing printed; any other character is printed as usual (EXT_DISP_PLAIN).","comment":"\"`\"?"},
        "1616": {"comment":"Remember it"},
        "1618": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_MAIN: prints nothing; the cursor's address off the stack.","comment":"Drop the cursor's address"},
        "1620": {"synopsis":"The extension's display driver (through EXT_DISP_DRIVER): DISPLAY_DRIVER's job, with the\n/64's special characters. Its flags are in $4036: bit 2 the special characters on, bit 3 a\n\"`\" waiting.\n1. Input (carry): DISP_READ.\n2. Special characters off: EXT_DISP_PLAIN.\n3. On: a \"`\" is held back (EXT_ACC_PREFIX); the next character picks the special one\n   (EXT_ACC_LETTER), \"``\" prints \"`\" itself.\nDisplay codes $15 and $14 switch them on and off (EXT_DISP_CTRL), as CLEAR+SHIFT+1 and +2 do\nfrom the keyboard.\nEntry: IX = the block, C = the character, carry set for input.\nExit: as DISPLAY_DRIVER.\nObserved: with PRINT CHR$(21) first, typing `a`3`#``X`A`z showed $10 $04 $14 $60 $58 $00\n(`z: nothing); without it, the characters as they are. Typing aBc stored $61 $42 $63.","comment":"HL = the cursor's address"},
        "1626": {"comment":"Input: the character at the cursor"},
        "1630": {"comment":"The flags"},
        "1633": {"comment":"A = the character"},
        "1634": {"comment":"Special characters on?"},
        "1638": {"comment":"A \"`\" waiting?"},
        "1642": {"comment":"Not any more"},
        "1644": {"comment":"\"``\" prints \"`\""},
        "1648": {"comment":"The special character"},
        "1651": {"synopsis":"Finds A in EXT_ACCENT_KEYS and prints the special character for it: its place 0-15 for a\ncapital (bit 5 of the kept character clear), the place + $10 for lower case, stored by\nDISP_WRITE. Not in the table: nothing is printed.\nEntry: A = the character, lower case; the character as typed on the stack (as BC).","comment":"The table"},
        "1654": {"comment":"16 characters"},
        "1657": {"comment":"Look for A"},
        "1659": {"comment":"E = the character as typed"},
        "1660": {"comment":"Not there: print nothing"},
        "1662": {"comment":"The place: 15 - what is left"},
        "1665": {"comment":"Lower case?"},
        "1667": {"comment":"A spare byte"},
        "1671": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_MAIN: \"``\": the \"`\" is printed as a plain character.","comment":"C = \"`\""},
        "1672": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_MAIN: an ordinary character. Puts back the character under the cursor (byte 5 of\nthe block, if not 0), then EXT_DISP_OUTPUT.","comment":"HL = the cursor's address"},
        "1673": {"comment":"The character under the cursor"},
        "1676": {"comment":"One kept?"},
        "1679": {"comment":"Put it back"},
        "1680": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_PLAIN: output, by the code in C: $00-$1F EXT_DISP_CTRL, $20-$7F stored as they are\n(DISP_WRITE), $80-$FF DISP_CODE_80.","comment":"The character"},
        "1681": {"comment":"A control code?"},
        "1685": {"comment":"$20-$7F stored as they are"},
        "1690": {"comment":"Graphics and spaces"},
        "1693": {"synopsis":"EXT_ACC_LOOKUP: lower case adds $10."},
        "1695": {"comment":"Lower case: $10-$1F"},
        "1697": {"synopsis":"EXT_ACC_LOOKUP: stores the special character's code at the cursor (DISP_WRITE).","comment":"HL = the cursor's address"},
        "1698": {"comment":"Store it"},
        "1707": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_OUTPUT for $00-$1F: $14 switches the special characters off and $15 on (bit 2 of\n$4036), and print nothing; the others are DISP_CONTROL's.\nEntry: A = C = the code, HL = the cursor's address.","comment":"DE = the cursor's address"},
        "1708": {"comment":"The flags"},
        "1739": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_CTRL: $14 switches the special characters off.","comment":"$14?"},
        "1743": {"comment":"Special characters off"},
        "1746": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_CTRL: $15 switches them on; any other code goes to DISP_CONTROL.","comment":"$15?"},
        "1750": {"comment":"HL = the cursor's address again"},
        "1751": {"comment":"BASIC's control codes"},
        "1754": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_CTRL: $15, the special characters on.","comment":"Special characters on"},
        "1757": {"synopsis":"EXT_NEW_KEY: the key's number (A) becomes the key last taken (IY+1), then EXT_KEY_DECODE.","comment":"The key last taken"},
        "1760": {"synopsis":"On to EXT_KEY_DECODE.","comment":"Decode it"},
        "1763": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_DOWN: a repeat; IY+5 = 1 (with IY+4 = $60: 96 passes to the next), then the key is\ndecoded.","comment":"The next repeat's high byte"},
        "1769": {"synopsis":"EXT_KEY_SCAN: no key down; IY+5 = 1 and IY+1 = $FF, no key last taken. Returns with Z.","comment":"Count reset"},
        "1773": {"comment":"No key last taken"},
        "1778": {"synopsis":"EXT_DISP_MAIN: the character after \"`\". Made lower case; a letter (and @ [ \\ ] ^ _ in their\nlower-case form) goes straight to EXT_ACC_FIND; anything else to EXT_ACC_DIGIT.\nEntry: A = C = the character.","comment":"Lower case"},
        "1780": {"comment":"A letter?"},
        "1785": {"comment":"A digit or symbol"},
        "1792": {"synopsis":"\"HIRADASTECHNIKA SZOVETKEZET  COPYRIGHT 06/03/1985 --- BUDAPEST  V2.51\" and spaces to $374F:\nthe maker, a date and the version, as text in the ROM. No code reads it (no operand points\ninto EXT_MAKER_TEXT-$37FF), and it has no closing zero."}
      }
    }
  },
